1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,120
Good afternoon. Today's lecture compares
five renewable energy storage technologies.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,889
Pumped hydro, lithium-ion
batteries, compressed

3
00:00:08,913 --> 00:00:11,512
air, flywheels, and
hydrogen storage.

4
00:00:11,970 --> 00:00:14,980
For each one, I'll explain
how it stores energy,

5
00:00:15,004 --> 00:00:18,152
its main limitation, a
project associated with it,

6
00:00:18,405 --> 00:00:20,509
and when the key
developments happened, so

7
00:00:20,533 --> 00:00:22,852
listen carefully as I go
through them in turn.

8
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:26,580
Let's begin with how each
technology actually stores energy.

9
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:30,160
Pumped hydro stores
energy as water held at height.

10
00:00:30,605 --> 00:00:34,000
When demand is low, water is
pumped uphill into a reservoir,

11
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:36,980
and it's released back down
through turbines when it's needed.

12
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,426
Flywheels store energy as a
spinning mass, and because

13
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:43,172
that mass can release its
energy almost instantly,

14
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,220
flywheels are the technology that
responds fastest to sudden demand.

15
00:00:47,605 --> 00:00:49,600
Lithium-ion batteries, you'll already know.

16
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:52,242
They're the technology
most common in electric

17
00:00:52,266 --> 00:00:54,392
vehicles, as well as
in phones and laptops.

18
00:00:54,819 --> 00:00:57,555
Compressed air storage
works by pumping air into

19
00:00:57,579 --> 00:01:00,212
an underground cavern,
often an old salt mine,

20
00:01:00,549 --> 00:01:03,480
and then releasing it through a
turbine when electricity is needed.

21
00:01:03,875 --> 00:01:06,225
And hydrogen storage
stands out because it can

22
00:01:06,249 --> 00:01:08,612
be used for long-distance
transport of energy.

23
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:11,811
Hydrogen can be shipped
in tankers or sent through

24
00:01:11,835 --> 00:01:14,552
pipelines to places other
technologies can't reach.

25
00:01:14,939 --> 00:01:18,300
Now, each technology also
has a significant limitation.

26
00:01:18,804 --> 00:01:20,900
Pumped hydro is limited by geography.

27
00:01:21,399 --> 00:01:23,969
You need hills or mountains
and plenty of water,

28
00:01:23,993 --> 00:01:26,252
so it simply can't be
built just anywhere.

29
00:01:26,674 --> 00:01:29,940
People sometimes raise safety
concerns about lithium-ion batteries,

30
00:01:30,404 --> 00:01:33,440
but in my view the real
limitation is material scarcity.

31
00:01:33,945 --> 00:01:36,280
Lithium and cobalt are in limited supply.

32
00:01:36,759 --> 00:01:39,220
Flywheels suffer from
short storage duration.

33
00:01:39,669 --> 00:01:41,775
They're excellent for
smoothing out a brief

34
00:01:41,799 --> 00:01:44,212
surge, but they can only
hold energy for minutes.

35
00:01:44,594 --> 00:01:46,820
Hydrogen's main problem is low efficiency.

36
00:01:47,270 --> 00:01:51,460
So much energy is lost converting
electricity into hydrogen and back again.

37
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:52,760
And compressed air?

38
00:01:53,094 --> 00:01:55,414
Suitable caverns exist
in more places than

39
00:01:55,438 --> 00:01:57,992
you'd think, so the main
problem is high cost.

40
00:01:58,309 --> 00:02:00,720
These plants are extremely
expensive to build.

41
00:02:01,070 --> 00:02:04,640
Let's now look at some real-world
projects, one for each technology.

42
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:08,010
The largest pumped hydro
plant in our case studies is

43
00:02:08,034 --> 00:02:10,952
in Norway, a country with
thousands of mountain lakes.

44
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,351
A major hydrogen export
project is being developed in

45
00:02:14,375 --> 00:02:17,832
Australia, which plans to ship
hydrogen to customers in Asia.

46
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:20,958
Germany ran a large-scale
battery storage trial

47
00:02:20,982 --> 00:02:23,572
to help balance the
output from its wind farms.

48
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:27,145
Chile has an ambitious
solar-plus storage desert project

49
00:02:27,169 --> 00:02:30,292
in the Atacama, providing
power well into the evening.

50
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:34,079
And one of the earliest flywheel
research programs was run

51
00:02:34,103 --> 00:02:37,412
in Japan, long before flywheels
became commercially viable.

52
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,520
Finally, let's put all
of this on a timeline.

53
00:02:40,984 --> 00:02:45,620
Pumped hydro is an old idea, but in the
National Grid we'll be modelling this term,

54
00:02:46,025 --> 00:02:49,740
the first commercial pumped
hydro plants opened in the 1980s.

55
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:53,378
The early lithium-ion battery
patents we'll focus on, the

56
00:02:53,402 --> 00:02:56,992
ones that made commercial
sales possible, date from the 1990s.

57
00:02:57,269 --> 00:03:01,720
The first grid-scale battery storage
installations then appeared in the 2000s,

58
00:03:01,945 --> 00:03:05,400
as utilities began experimenting
with storing renewable output.

59
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:09,337
The 2010s saw a rapid fall in
battery costs, which is really

60
00:03:09,361 --> 00:03:12,812
what made large-scale storage
commercially viable at last.

61
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:17,920
And most recently, the 2020s have brought
large-scale green hydrogen projects.

62
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,930
Right, let's now compare
the overall efficiency

63
00:03:20,954 --> 00:03:23,592
of these five technologies
in a bit more detail.

